Thomas B Valesky (tvalesky@mason1.gmu.edu) wrote:
> I've spent more time playing Nethack than on any 10 other games
> combined. However, when I try to explain the wonders of Nethack to an
> unbeliever, I can't seem to put the reasons for my love of the game to
> them. Anybody care to try to write a poem or something about "Why
> Nethack is the greatest game in the history of the human race?"
Sorry, no poem, but I like NetHack because ...
	1.) Every game is different because of the randomly generated
	    levels.
	2.) There always are surprises or things you didn't know of
	    before.
	3.) It is incredibly elaborate. The DevTeam seems to think
	    of every single detail. Wishing for rocks? Sure! Zapping
	    yourself with a wand of death? Of course! Throwing your
	    Stormbringer on your own head? Why not?
	4.) The source code is available, which means that you can
	    study, understand and even enhance the game on your own.
	5.) The game has the right balance between strategy and
	    simple "hack away" (no pun intended).
	6.) Strange as it may seem, it even stimulates your phantasy.
	    How do you imagine a 'J' (Jabberwock) or the Wizard of
	    Yendor to look like? 

Just my $0.02 worth ... 
